<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- ADDED 19/8/2026. Without this every file dialog on macOS dies with

	         PlatformException(ENTITLEMENT_NOT_FOUND, Either the Read-Only or
	         Read-Write entitlement is required for this action.)

	     thrown from FilePickerMacOS.pickFiles. com.apple.security.app-sandbox
	     is true above, and a sandboxed app may not open NSOpenPanel/NSSavePanel
	     unless it declares that the user is allowed to hand it files. This is
	     what broke "Upload Watermark" on macOS.

	     read-write, not read-only: the same entitlement governs NSSavePanel,
	     and the app also writes files the user chooses a location for — PDF
	     conversion (f8_convert_pdf) and the CSV export in f6_result. read-only
	     would fix picking and leave saving broken in the same way. -->
	<!-- ADDED 2/9/2026. Voice notes in the chat composer. Without this, a
	     sandboxed app cannot open an input device at all: RecorderController
	     .record() fails and the composer's mic button does nothing, with no
	     system permission prompt shown (the sandbox denies before macOS ever
	     asks). NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist supplies the text of
	     that prompt; both are required. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- ADDED 9/9/2026. Activity logging records where an action happened.
	     Because com.apple.security.app-sandbox is true above, a sandboxed app
	     cannot reach Core Location at all without this — the sandbox denies
	     before macOS ever shows a prompt, exactly as it did for the microphone.
	     NSLocationUsageDescription / NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription in
	     Info.plist supply the text of that prompt; both are required. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- ADDED 27/8/2026. Without this the "Download" action on macOS dies with

	         PathAccessException: Cannot create file, path =
	         '.../Library/Containers/com.knowticed.plus/Data/Downloads/<file>'
	         (OS Error: Operation not permitted, errno = 1)

	     thrown from _downloadFileDesktop (knowledge_hub_file_operations.dart).
	     Because com.apple.security.app-sandbox is true, path_provider's
	     getDownloadsDirectory() is redirected into the app container's
	     Data/Downloads, and the sandbox then denies the write. This entitlement
	     grants the sandboxed app access to the user's real ~/Downloads AND makes
	     macOS redirect getDownloadsDirectory() to it, so the file lands in the
	     actual Downloads folder and the write succeeds. Unlike
	     user-selected.read-write, it needs no save panel — the download stays
	     silent, which is the intended UX. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.downloads.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- REMOVED 13/8/2026: keychain-access-groups.

	     It was added for flutter_secure_storage, but Xcode refuses to build a
	     target carrying it unless that target signs with a real development
	     certificate:

	         error: "Runner" has entitlements that require signing with a
	         development certificate. Enable development signing in the
	         Signing & Capabilities editor.

	     This project signs ad-hoc — CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = "-", CODE_SIGN_STYLE =
	     Manual, no DEVELOPMENT_TEAM — so the macOS debug build failed outright.

	     Removing it costs nothing, because the entitlement never worked under
	     ad-hoc signing in the first place: with no team, $(AppIdentifierPrefix)
	     resolves to nothing and every Keychain call already failed with
	     errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018). SecureCredentialStore documents this
	     exact case and degrades to normal sign-in, so the only effect is that
	     biometric / saved-password login does not persist in a macOS debug
	     build. iOS, Android and Windows are unaffected, and
	     Release.entitlements still declares the group for signed builds.

	     If macOS is later signed with a real team, restore:

	         <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	         <array>
	           <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.knowticed.plus</string>
	         </array>
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
